the concept of washington's revolving  door goes like this administration  officials from both parties often leave  office and step into lucrative positions  often in industries they used to lead or  regulate and a new study from columbia  university's brown institute for media  innovation muckrock and forbes found  that the potential earnings for these  arrangements can be massive  for years we know from magnetic thoughts  that politicians can make a lot of money  by going through the revolving door  but nobody knows how much  we at the brown institute at columbia  university tracked more than 1100  officials in the buying administration  among them we found 151 who qualified as  revolving door officials  that is they left the government in the  final days of the obama administration  worked for the private sector and then  returned undivided for 77 of these  officials were able to obtain their  before and after financial disclosures  and we found a 270 percent increase in  the reported minimum assets  in comparison  u.s household assets increased 38  from 2017 to 2021 and the s p 500 went  up 70 over the same period  so we can see these officials made a lot  more money than average amount  there are three major ways people made  money going through the revolving door  the first is to join a big company and  earn a big salary  i consider defense secretary loy austin  after he retired as four-star general in  2016  austin joined the boards of race and  a major military contractor new core  corporation one of the biggest steel  producers in the u.s antenna healthcare  he made more than one billion dollars in  cash and 2 million dollars in equities  the second way for those who don't work  directly for a big company is to join a  law firm or lobbying for example beth  george a lawyer in the defense and  justice departments under obama headed  to a silicon valley law firm where she  made 1.8 million dollars in 2020 working  for companies such as google twitter and  stamp  the third way is to start your own shop  then deputy secretary of state anthony  blinken co-founded a strategic  consulting firm called red exact  advisors  where he had clients such as fedex  blackstone and uber three years later as  secretary of state he listed 1.2 million  dollars of guaranteed payments and  distributions from west exec  and over 1.5 million dollars of  investments connected to his firm he's  now worth an estimated 10 million  dollars  representatives from the small business  administration office of the director of  national intelligence the white house  and state department said in statements  that their employees face routine and  stringent ethic requirements while in  office  on the day he became president joe biden  issued an executive order that banned  officials from working on some issues  like regulations and contracts that are  directly related to their former  employers and clients the ban however  only applies to cases where a personal  service has been provided to the company  in question which also has to be a named  party in the policy discussion  in reality much of what a corporation  wants from the government is more  abstract for example broad tax relief  for general trade policy  what we found most interesting  is both republicans and democrats claim  to hate this one  however members from both parties  benefit from it and we picked these  obama abiding officials not because we  thought one party is worse than the  other rather this is the only data  currently available to us  and we wanted to shed light  on this important issue of government  ethics in washington  [Music]  you
 

